course: dictionary
lesson: 5110
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words can do more than one job in a sentence. Interview can be a noun, which names a thing, or a verb, which is an action. «A job interview is a meeting where an employer asks questions.» Here an interview is the meeting itself, so it is a noun.
explain: «Reporters often pick dramatic people to interview.» Here interview is something reporters do: they ask people questions. That makes it a verb. Ask yourself: is this word naming something, or doing something?
explain: The words around interview show you which meaning you need. It can mean a job meeting, a school task or a recorded chat for TV, radio or a newspaper. Check the clues before you decide.
fact: Interview can be a noun and a verb.
fact: As a noun, an interview is a meeting where someone asks questions, for a job, a school place or a news story.
fact: As a verb, to interview someone means to ask them questions to find out about them.
fact: An interview can also be a conversation recorded for TV, radio or a newspaper.
fact: Phrases to notice: «a job interview», «give an interview», «interview someone about».
exercise: «A job interview is a meeting where an employer asks questions to decide whom to hire.» What is an interview here? | A letter sent to an employer | *A meeting where someone asks questions | A kind of clothing | A type of job
exercise: «Reporters often pick dramatic people to interview.» What does interview mean here? | To write a letter about someone | To sing to a crowd | To take a photo of someone | *To ask someone questions to find out about them
exercise: Ella will interview her grandad for her history homework. What does interview mean here? | *To ask him questions to find out about his life | To give him a present | To watch him play football | To write his name on a card
test: Finding a job can take time: an interview is a meeting to see if you get the job. In this sentence, what is an interview? | *A meeting to decide if you get the job | A type of school | A form you fill in | A kind of job
test: «She gave an interview to the local radio.» Is interview a noun or a verb here? | *Noun | Adjective | Verb | Adverb
exam: Which sentence uses interview as a verb? | She went to a job interview | *Reporters often pick people to interview | Hear the interview on the radio | A job interview is on Monday
